  "html": "<div id=\"questionExplanation-119705\" class=\"questionExplanation\" style=\"display: block;\"><p>Among the given options, the <em>dodecahedron</em> is shown in the following picture:</p>\n<div id=\"graphic-q-119705-a-1\" class=\"graphic\"><img src=\"/graphics/q-119705-a-1\"></div>\n\n<p>A dodecahedron is a regular polyhedron that </p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>consists of <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"1\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> congruent faces, each in the form of a regular pentagon, and</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>exactly <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"2\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c33\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> faces meet at each vertex.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Recall that there are only five types of Platonic solids (convex regular polyhedra):</p>\n<div id=\"graphic-q-119705-e-1\" class=\"graphic\"><img src=\"/graphics/q-119705-e-1\"></div> \n</div>",
